<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cyanide detoxification I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="ASPSYNII-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: ETHYLENE-CMPD Ethylene| is an important plant hormone that regulates growth and development. It is well-known as a fruit-ripening hormone and is biologically active in trace amounts. Ethylene also plays a general role as a growth inhibitor in promoting leaf and flower senescence and abscission. |FRAME:HCN Hydrogen cyanide| is formed in plants in stoichiometrically equal amounts to ethylene as a byproduct of ethylene biosynthesis (see |FRAME:ETHYL-PWY|) |CITS:[16593463]| |CITS:[Grossmann03]|. Cyanide is toxic to all living cells and requires rapid detoxification in the plant tissues |CITS: [3073064]| |CITS: [12422245]|. Furthermore, as a defense mechanism, cyanide is also released from cyanogenic glucosides, i.e. cyanogenesis, by cyanogenic plants (see for example |FRAME:PWY-7089|, |FRAME:PWY-7093|). Cyanide is detoxified to |FRAME:CPD-603|, a non-protein amino acid which is wide-spread in higher plants and is neurotoxic to many animals. In most plants, |FRAME:CPD-603| can be further metabolized to |FRAME:ASN| and |FRAME:L-ASPARTATE| (this pathway) |CITS:[11060302]|. There is also evidence that |FRAME:HCN| plays a regulatory (maybe signaling) function in physiological processes (|CITS:[Siegien06]|, e.g. seed germination (|CITS:[Bogatek99]| |CITS:[18448476]| |CITS:[Gniazdowska10]|), nitrate assimilation (|CITS:[Solomonson90]|) or in plant responses to stress and other environmental factors (|CITS:[Grossmann96]| |CITS:[21075959]|). Since the early 1960s, studies have shown that C-14 labeled hydrocyanic acid can be incorporated to |FRAME:ASN| in the seedlings of an array of plants, including sorghum, barley, pea, flax, and soybean |CITS: [4798760]|. Enzymes that catalyze the two sequential reactions, &amp;beta;-cyanoalanine synthase and &amp;beta;-cyanoalanine hydrolase have been isolated and their enzyme activities have been confirmed in |FRAME:TAX-3701| and other higher plants |CITS: [4627358][11060302][1055433][10845460][10889265] [16786295]|. A temporal and spatial association exists between ethylene synthesis and cyanide detoxifying activity (this pathway) indicating a semi-quantitative relationship between ACC oxidase and &amp;beta;-cyanoalanine synthase, the two key enzymes of the respective pathways |CITS: [3073064]|.</Description>
